on, which $\\rho$ and $\\theta$ cover"],["line","r","$\\rho$ constant, at $L/2$, $L$, $3L/2$ and $2L$: the circle of Euclidean time"],["line","reference","the light cone of the Minkowski space it is drawn in, which the sheet nears as $\\rho \\to \\infty$"],["line","meridian","$\\theta$ constant, every $15°$"]],"turn":{"origins":[[0.0,0.0]],"meridians":24,"tint":{"sheet":"cover"},"marks":[]}},"settings":"$L = 1$, the unit of every length, and $r_h = L$, on the surface of $\\rho$ and the angle $\\theta = ir_hct/L^2$. Every length along the sheet is measured with $dX^2 + dY^2 - dZ^2$.","stops":["At every $\\rho > 0$ the circles grow faster than the distance out to them, as $\\cosh(\\rho/L)$, and no surface of revolution in flat space carries the disc; Minkowski space carries it."],"caption":["The Euclidean section of the black hole, drawn as a surface in three dimensional Minkowski space with every distance along it the metric distance. With the time turned to the angle $\\theta = ir_hct/L^2$ the metric is $d\\rho^2 + L^2\\sinh^2(\\rho/L)\\,d\\theta^2$, the hyperbolic plane of curvature $-1/L^2$, so the circle at $\\rho$ has circumference $2\\pi L\\sinh(\\rho/L)$.","The circles grow faster than the distance out to them, so the disc stands in Minkowski space, on the sheet $Z = L\\cosh(\\rho/L) - L$ of a hyperboloid that nears the light cone far out. At the horizon $\\rho = 0$ the circle closes to a point as smoothly as the pole of a sphere, which fixes the period $2\\pi$ of $\\theta$, a period $2\\pi L^2/r_hc$ of the Euclidean time, and its inverse is the Hawking temperature $\\hbar c\\,r_h/2\\pi k_BL^2$.","The meridians $\\theta = 0$ and $\\theta = \\pi$ are the moment $t = 0$ on the two sides of the horizon, the line along which the Euclidean section meets the black hole."]}]}
